\subsection{GDALMDArrayR class}{

Objects of class \code{GDALMDArrayR} are obtained from \code{ds$openArray()},
\code{grp$openMDArray()}, or the various view/transform methods. An MDArray
is a named multidimensional array with associated dimensions, data type,
attributes, spatial reference, and optional CF convention properties
(nodata, scale, offset, unit).
\subsection{Properties}{

\verb{$isValid()}\cr
Returns logical \code{TRUE} if the array handle is valid.

\verb{$getName()}\cr
Returns the array name.

\verb{$getFullName()}\cr
Returns the fully qualified array name.

\verb{$getTotalElementsCount()}\cr
Returns the total number of elements in the array.

\verb{$getDimensionCount()}\cr
Returns the number of dimensions.

\verb{$getDimensions()}\cr
Returns a list of \code{GDALDimensionR} objects describing each dimension.

\verb{$getDataType()}\cr
Returns a \code{GDALExtendedDataTypeR} object describing the array data type.

\verb{$getSpatialRef()}\cr
Returns the spatial reference system as a WKT string, or \code{""} if not set.

\verb{$setSpatialRef(wkt)}\cr
Sets the spatial reference from a WKT string. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$getUnit()}\cr
Returns the unit string (e.g., \code{"m"}, \code{"K"}, \code{"days since 1970-01-01"}),
or \code{""} if not set.

\verb{$setUnit(unit)}\cr
Sets the unit string. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$getNoDataValueAsDouble()}\cr
Returns the nodata value as a length-1 numeric vector, or length-0 if
no nodata value is set.

\verb{$getNoDataValueAsRaw()}\cr
Returns the nodata value as raw bytes.

\verb{$setNoDataValue(nodata)}\cr
Sets the nodata value as a double. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$setNoDataValueRaw(nodata)}\cr
Sets the nodata value from a raw vector. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$deleteNoDataValue()}\cr
Deletes the nodata value. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$getOffset()}\cr
Returns the offset value as a length-1 numeric vector, or length-0 if
not set. Used with \verb{$getScale()} for CF convention decoding:
\code{value = raw_value * scale + offset}.

\verb{$getScale()}\cr
Returns the scale value as a length-1 numeric vector, or length-0 if
not set.

\verb{$setOffset(offset)}\cr
Sets the offset value. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$setScale(scale)}\cr
Sets the scale value. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$getBlockSize()}\cr
Returns a numeric vector giving the block (chunk) size for each
dimension. A value of \code{0} indicates that the dimension is not chunked.

\verb{$getProcessingChunkSize(maxChunkMemory)}\cr
Returns a recommended chunk size for processing, given a memory budget
in bytes.

\verb{$getStructuralInfo()}\cr
Returns driver-specific structural information.
}

\subsection{Attributes}{

\verb{$getAttributeNames()}\cr
Returns a character vector of attribute names on the array.

\verb{$getAttribute(name)}\cr
Returns a \code{GDALAttributeR} object.

\verb{$getAttributes()}\cr
Returns a list of all \code{GDALAttributeR} objects.

\verb{$createAttribute(name, dimensions, dataType, options)}\cr
Creates a new attribute on the array.

\verb{$deleteAttribute(name, options)}\cr
Deletes an attribute. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.
}

\subsection{I/O}{

\verb{$read(arrayStartIdx, count, arrayStep, bufferStride, bufferDataType)}\cr
Read array data as an R vector or array. All parameters are optional.
\code{arrayStartIdx} is a numeric vector of 0-based start indices for each
dimension. \code{count} is a numeric vector of element counts per dimension
(default: read all remaining). \code{arrayStep} is a numeric vector of step
sizes per dimension (default: 1). \code{bufferStride} is a numeric vector of
buffer strides (default: \code{NULL} for C-style row-major). \code{bufferDataType}
is a \code{GDALExtendedDataTypeR} to request type conversion during read.

For arrays with more than one dimension, the returned R object has a
\code{dim} attribute set. \strong{Note:} GDAL uses row-major (C) dimension ordering,
while R uses column-major (Fortran) ordering. The \verb{$read()} method
reverses the dimension order so that the result follows R conventions,
with the first GDAL dimension becoming the last R dimension.

The return type depends on the data type: integer vector/array for
\code{Byte}, \code{Int8}, \code{Int16}, \code{UInt16}, \code{Int32}; numeric vector/array for
\code{Float32}, \code{Float64}, \code{UInt32}, \code{Int64}, \code{UInt64}; character
vector/array for string types; and raw vector for compound types (with
an \code{element_size} attribute).

\verb{$write(data, arrayStartIdx, count, arrayStep, bufferStride, bufferDataType)}\cr
Write data to the array. \code{data} is an R numeric, integer, character, or
raw vector. The remaining parameters have the same meaning as in
\verb{$read()}. Requires write access. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$adviseRead(arrayStartIdx, count, options)}\cr
Notify the driver of an upcoming read region for optimization. Some
drivers (e.g., netCDF via DAP) use this to pre-cache data. Returns
\code{TRUE} on success.
}

\subsection{Views and transforms}{

\verb{$getView(viewExpr)}\cr
Returns a new \code{GDALMDArrayR} that is a view of this array using
NumPy-style basic slicing and indexing syntax. For example,
\code{"[0:10,...]"}, \code{"[:,::2]"}, or \code{"['field_name']"}. See
\code{\link[=mdim_as_classic]{mdim_as_classic()}} for the full view expression syntax.
The returned array is a view (not a copy) and holds a reference to the
original.

\verb{$transpose(mapNewAxisToOldAxis)}\cr
Returns a transposed view of the array. \code{mapNewAxisToOldAxis} is an
integer vector mapping each new axis position to its original position.
For example, to swap the first two dimensions of a 3D array:
\code{c(1L, 0L, 2L)}.

\verb{$getUnscaled()}\cr
Returns a view with scale and offset applied (unscaled values). The
returned array applies \code{value = raw * scale + offset} on read.

\verb{$getMask(options)}\cr
Returns a mask array where valid values are \code{1} and invalid (nodata)
values are \code{0}.

\verb{$getResampled(newDims, resampleAlg, targetSRS, options)}\cr
Returns a resampled and/or reprojected view. \code{newDims} is a list of
\code{GDALDimensionR} objects (or \code{NULL} entries for unchanged dimensions).
\code{resampleAlg} is one of \code{"nearest"}, \code{"bilinear"}, \code{"cubic"},
\code{"cubicspline"}, \code{"lanczos"}, \code{"average"}, \code{"rms"}, \code{"mode"},
\code{"gauss"}. \code{targetSRS} is a WKT SRS string (empty for no reprojection).

\verb{$asClassicDataset(iXDim, iYDim, rootGroup, options)}\cr
Returns a classic 2D \code{GDALRaster} object. \code{iXDim} and \code{iYDim} are
0-based dimension indices for the X and Y axes. For arrays with more
than 2 dimensions, additional dimensions are represented as raster bands.
\code{rootGroup} is an optional \code{GDALGroupR} object.
See also \code{\link[=mdim_as_classic]{mdim_as_classic()}}.
}

\subsection{Other}{

\verb{$cache(options)}\cr
Cache the array data (e.g., to speed up access to an expensive view such
as a transposed array). The cache is stored as a \code{.gmac} file alongside
the source. Returns \code{TRUE} on success.

\verb{$computeStatistics(approxOK, force, options)}\cr
Compute statistics. Returns a list with components \verb{$min}, \verb{$max},
\verb{$mean}, \verb{$stddev}, \verb{$valid_count}.

\verb{$resize(newDimSizes, options)}\cr
Resize the array to new dimension sizes. \code{newDimSizes} is a numeric
vector. Returns \code{TRUE} on success. Optionally supported.

\verb{$rename(newName)}\cr
Renames the array. Returns \code{TRUE} on success. Optionally supported.
}

}

\subsection{GDALDimensionR class}{

Objects of class \code{GDALDimensionR} are obtained from
\code{arr$getDimensions()} or \code{grp$getDimensions()}. A dimension has a name,
size, type, and direction, and may have an associated indexing variable
(a 1D array of coordinate values).

\verb{$isValid()}\cr
Returns \code{TRUE} if the dimension handle is valid.

\verb{$getName()}\cr
Returns the dimension name.

\verb{$getFullName()}\cr
Returns the fully qualified dimension name.

\verb{$getType()}\cr
Returns the dimension type as a character string. Possible values include
\code{"HORIZONTAL_X"}, \code{"HORIZONTAL_Y"}, \code{"VERTICAL"}, \code{"TEMPORAL"},
\code{"PARAMETRIC"}, or \code{""} (unknown).

\verb{$getDirection()}\cr
Returns the dimension direction (e.g., \code{"EAST"}, \code{"NORTH"}, \code{"UP"},
or \code{""}).

\verb{$getSize()}\cr
Returns the dimension size as a numeric value.

\verb{$getIndexingVariable()}\cr
Returns the indexing (coordinate) variable as a \code{GDALMDArrayR} object,
or \code{NULL} if no indexing variable is associated. The indexing variable
is a 1D array containing the coordinate values along this dimension.

\verb{$setIndexingVariable(array)}\cr
Sets the indexing variable. \code{array} is a \code{GDALMDArrayR} object. Returns
\code{TRUE} on success. Optionally supported.

\verb{$rename(newName)}\cr
Renames the dimension. Returns \code{TRUE} on success. Optionally supported.
}

